A||||24||fiero56||suspension squeak||||8||
Z||000000||fiero56||06-26-2000||03:38 PM||||I have just over 160,000 miles on the ('88)Formula now, and the bushings up front have been starting to go south for the last few months. A squeak just started last week(driving me crazy!), so I plan to replace all bushings soon.<BR>I have asked about polly bushings before, but we have several(hundred) new memembers since then. I am ready to buy and install polyurethane bushings for all four corners. The only reason I haven't bought them yet is I am afraid they will squeak too,<P>"These polyurethane bushings for the Fiero, are all made by Prothane. <BR>Prothane does not make graphite-impregnated polyurethane." <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A> <P>I would prefer to get graphite-impregnated bushings, but it looks like none are made for Fiero.<P>Question: Will these bushings squeak if installed straight out of the box? I don't want to have to screw around putting grease and crap on them.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Old Lar||06-26-2000||03:55 PM||||When you pay someone else to install the bushings you won't have to get your hands dirty at all.  My new ones squeak, but I only hear it at low speeds, with the radio off.  Once up to driving speed 35-40 mph, the exhaust sounds cover up the squeak.  The car handles better than ever with the new bushings.||||reg||4
Z||000002||fiero56||06-26-2000||04:09 PM||||You're saying the squeak isn't terribly loud then? Is this something people waking down the sidewalk would be able to hear?<P>My Fiero has more of a loud "chirp" than a squeak. It's pretty loud, and oh so annoying.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||06-26-2000||05:47 PM||||Fiero56,<P>A couple of other things to check out. <P>I had a very annoying (and loud) squeak in my 88 Formula. It turned out to be a bad bushing on the top shock mount. It looked OK from the outside but the inside part was torn and letting the shock shaft rub the mount.<P>Also, while trying to find my problem, I read somewhere that the 88s often develop a squeaking or chirping noise because of the front sway bar bushings (where it bolts to the chassis). Some silicon grease is supposed to solve that problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||06-26-2000||08:10 PM||||I replaced front (and rear) sway bar bushings about two months ago. The car used to chirp(unrelated to the sway bushings), it went away, and now has come back. I plan to replace the shocks at the same time(getting old).<BR>The stock rubber bushings everywhere are pretty bad(a lot bad)and I would like to restore the handling back to better than ever. The front end has really lost it's grip over the last several months.<P>If polyurethane doesn't squeak very loudly, I will be installing it soon. Does anyone make the graphite-impregnated bushings for Fiero? If not, I will end up having to buy those pretty red bushings, and have to paint my suspension too <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||06-26-2000||08:24 PM||||You have to lubricate your polyurethane bushings. If they are lubricated right then they should not make any noise. My brother has a rear sway bar on his 87 fiero and it has polyurethane bushings on it, which make no noise. They have a liberal amount of the special grease on them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||06-26-2000||08:34 PM||||Sway bars don't see as much movement, except for speed bumps and the like. LCAs and UCAs are the places I am especially concerned about squeaking. They see much more movement, and these bushings are quite worn on my Formula. Sway bar bushings on mine are poly right now(no squeaking).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||06-27-2000||02:48 AM||||I have poly bushings and I greased them with the special grease that came with them. The only time I hear my suspension squeek is speed bumps and pulling into my driveway (small curb) Also On cold mornings it does squeek a little until the suspension has "warmed up". (actually it isn't a squeek it's more of a creek)||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||06-27-2000||08:51 AM||||I'm convinced. I will go with polyurethane, and enjoy a much better handling Fiero. I can't wait.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phantom Rage||06-27-2000||11:09 AM||||Poly's are cool, but you can also get polygraphite.  They have graphite impregnated in the poly as a lubricant.  PST sells them, that is also where I got my rear sway bar.  You can at least get universal swaybar center bushing and endlinks from them, but not sure about any other bushings.  I greased my polygraphites anyway, just for good measure. I have not heard a peep from them yet||||reg||7
Z||000010||fiero56||06-27-2000||12:58 PM||||PST<P>Do they have a website? I am not familiar with them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||06-27-2000||01:12 PM||||I have Poly bushings on my car and there are no squeeks. I coated them with molybedemyum (sp)grease before their install back in october of 98...  if your really going full tilt Paeco makes Delrin bushings to order and Chris West is making rear needle bearings for the trailing arms...  no squeeks there!||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||06-27-2000||01:16 PM||||and another thing DON"T buy them from The Fiero Store! they are way over priced.  call Chris or Danny At NOPI  877-700-6674. I got my entire bushing set for less than half price including shipping compared to what the fiero store wanted... thats if you want Prothane. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||06-27-2000||01:57 PM||||I was planning to order mine from suspension.com. Is NOPI cheaper? I will try to give them a call tommorrow.<P>How difficult is it to install new bushings? I'm talking about the entire front end, and rear end('88 uses a tri-link setup in back, no lower control arm). Should I just find a reputable shop around town and have them do the work?||||reg||1
Z||000014||bHooper||06-27-2000||04:02 PM||||whats the NOPI part number's?  do they include the cradle bushings?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Phantom Rage||06-27-2000||06:32 PM||||Fiero56, This is their website  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <BR><A HREF="http://www.p-s-t.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.p-s-t.com/</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 06-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||1FST2M6||06-28-2000||08:44 AM||||Nopi part #s<P>7-502-b   cradle kit  ($37 + shipping)not $69<BR>7-304-b   trailing kit<BR>7-207-b   other trailing kit||||reg||1
Z||000017||RobertVol||06-28-2000||11:00 PM||||alot of times people think its the bushings squeeking when alot of time you can grease the tie rod ends and find out its that not the bushings. I replaced bushings and still heard that infernal squeek !! then greased the underarage of the car especially the tie rod ends and it stopped. just my couple of cents worth .||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||06-29-2000||12:30 AM||||Good point Robert. I'll pull out the grease gun and hit all the grease fittings. I would still like to go to poly though, control arms are a little loose.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Eric||06-29-2000||12:52 AM||||My 88's bushings are about gone too.  I'd really like an honest unbiased opinion about the feel and performance of the poly bushing on the 88.  <P>Low CG said he cuts grooves in the bushings to hold extra grease.  I'm not sure how he does this, but he said it stops the squeaking by constantly supplying the surface of the bushing with grease.  A lot of custom suspension setups that use poly bushings have grease fittings directly over the bushing and those never have squeaking problems.<P>What brand of shocks are you going with?  I thought seriously about Konis, but they are a bit too pricy for me right now, so I think Monroe and KYB are the only other options.  My shocks are going too.  It's getting a little dangerous so I have to make a decision quickly.  Right now I have KYBs on the front and Monroes on the back and I don't know which is worse.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Taijiguy||06-29-2000||01:22 AM||||For center link bushings, use a Dremel or something to grind a narrow, shallow groove around the perimeter, and then drill a hole in the mount, and tap it to recieve a grease fitting. Then let Jiffy-lube squirt some grease in there every 3000 miles with your oil change.||||reg||3
Z||000021||fiero56||07-02-2000||07:21 PM||||I am going to shop around town tommorrow to see who is willing (and able)to install poly bushings for me. I don't do suspension work, unless I have an impact set(I don't, yet). <P>Should I put the "grooves" in the bushings before I have them installed, or just have them installed straight out of the box with no "grooves"? As soon as I have them installed, I will report any change in handling.||||reg||1
Z||000022||fiero56||07-07-2000||12:53 PM||||I gave NOPI a call today. I spoke with David. He didn't give me a very good impression of that place.<BR>conversation whent something like this:<P>(me)I need to order polyurethane suspension bushings for a 1988 Pontiac Fiero.<BR>(him) what year (me)1988 (him)what kind (me)pontiac (him)how do you spell that (me)p-o-n-t-i-a-c (him)what car (me)Fiero (him)how do you spell that (me)f-i-e-r-o  (him)what year (me) 1988 (him)what did you need; it goes on like that for about 5 minutes, then he says they don't carry polyurethane bushings, just stock rubber ones. He told me they don't deal with polyurethane for ANY car, not just the Fiero.<P>I didn't have the part numbers with me, so I just let it go. I think I'll go with suspension.com(much more friendly, and the guy actually knew how to spell pontiac <B>and Fiero</B> amazing!!!||||reg||1
Z||000023||theogre||07-07-2000||04:35 PM||||read all the stuff posted at <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A>  and don't be affraid to call him. I've dealt with him. nice guy when he's not swamped, then he just doesn't have allot of time to talk.<P>there are several methods to reduce and eliminate polly squeak. (Graphite actually doesn't help that problem much. Doesn't get enough graphite in contact with what squeaks. Wrong amount of graphite in compound can weakn the polly matrix.)<P>there are a few things that can be done. you can do them together for future greasing.<P>1. install Zirk fitings. Put the bushing in place and drill a whole thru the shell and bushing. tap the shell and install the fitting. make sure the fitting is placed so you can get to it on the car and it wont get hit by anything. between the A arm rails is a good area.<P>2. tap the bushing. Good for an install time option but makes regreasing a pain.<P>3. CLEAN all areas the bushing will contact on sway bars and frame pockets. <P>If sway bars are dirty or rough it won't matter how much grease you put in the center bushings. Clean a wider area than the old bushings ran on. Polish the area as best you can.<P>If frame pockets are rusty and rough consider making large washers to go between the round faces of the bushings and the pockets. thin Brass, Stainless, Low Density Pollyethelene, or Teflon sheet will all work. If the round faces of the bushings are rubbing the frame pockets you get the same result as no grease inside the bushing core. When you make the washers make the center hole to float free around the core sleeve.||||reg||1
Z||000024||fiero56||07-08-2000||12:07 AM||||I called suspension.com today. They guy was friendly and knew what he was talking about. I would have ordered the bushings right then, but I don't want to buy $150 worth of bushings, and not know what to do with them. I will order as soon as I find a shop I feel comfortable with doing the work for me at a price I can justify. I will pay the $500+ if I have to, but I would prefer to keep the project a little lower. I am just a college student.<P>Good information there Ogre. I learn something new everyday. Thanks for the info. I will keep it in mind when having the bushings installed.||||reg||1
